-**Exit codes of the scan** (from the author's own runs with the pinned Semgrep 1.185.0; no log is shipped, except that the retry of exit 2 is tested in `tests/test_nosemgrep_form.py`): 0 = no finding; 1 = at least one finding (the job prints "findings above" and fails; findings are never retried); 2 = the registry could not be reached or a scan path does not exist (the job tries up to three times in all, 20 seconds apart, then fails); 7 = a rule file or address that cannot be read; any other non-zero code is a tool error (the job prints that it is not a finding). 3 = some files that cannot be parsed, when `--strict` is on (as the job has it). Measured with Semgrep 1.185.0, a made-up rule and no network: a Python or a JavaScript file with an unclosed call bracket (`print("hit"` or `eval("hit"`, alone or after a broken `def` or `function` line) made Semgrep exit 3 with `--strict` and exit 0 without it, and the job would then fail with the message "not findings" (it is not retried). The share of lines read does not predict this: it was about 0 to 100 percent in those files. Not every syntax error does it: `def f(:` followed by `  pass`, and `function f( {` followed by `}`, were read in full (about 100 percent) and gave exit 0 even with `--strict`. When another file has a real finding, the exit code is 1. So some files that Semgrep cannot read make the job fail: this is how `--strict` behaves, not a finding. A run of the same kind (also the author's own; no log is shipped) on the five folders above (a made-up rule for Python and JavaScript, not the registry packs, 47 files on 61ded9e plus this change) gave exit 0 with and without `--strict` and about 100 percent of lines read; that can change when the files change. The rules are fetched live and cannot be pinned, so the registry can change what this check reports without any change here: keep it out of the required checks, and if the job fails with exit 2 after three tries, re-run it later.
+**Exit codes of the scan** as the workflow handles them (`.github/workflows/semgrep.yml`): 0 = the job passes; 1 = the job prints "findings above" and fails (findings are never retried); 2 = the job tries again, up to three tries in all, 20 seconds apart, then fails (this retry is tested in `tests/test_nosemgrep_form.py`); any other non-zero code is printed as a tool or network error, not a finding, and the job fails. The rules are fetched live and cannot be pinned, so the registry can change what this check reports without any change here: keep it out of the required checks, and if the job fails with exit 2 after three tries, re-run it later.
 
